{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011ThXaRBf3PbFPXnn9SUau6","type":"tool_result","content":"package worker\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/closeout\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/committer\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/github\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tmaxQuoteRunes     = 4000\n\tsummaryWithheld   = \"Summary withheld by the secret scan.\"\n\tsummaryNone       = \"The agent wrote no summary.\"\n\ttestsRanOutOfTime = \"ran out of time\"\n)\n\n// Title is the PR title: the first line of the code commit message.\nfunc Title(typ, issueTitle string, issue int, jobID string) (string, error) {\n\tmsg, err := committer.CodeMessage(typ, issueTitle, issue, \"\", jobID)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\tfirst, _, _ := strings.Cut(msg, \"\\n\")\n\treturn first, nil\n}\n\n// QuoteSummary renders agent text for a PR body: closing references\n// neutralised (including the HTML-entity and backslash forms of \"#\"), ard\n// markers and @-mentions defused, cut to 4,000 runes and quoted. The text must\n// already be redacted.\nfunc QuoteSummary(s string) string {\n\ts = strings.TrimSpace(strings.ReplaceAll(s, \"\\r\", \"\"))\n\tif s == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"> \" + summaryNone\n\t}\n\ts = closeout.Sanitize(committer.NeutraliseRefs(s))\n\tif r := []rune(s); len(r) > maxQuoteRunes {\n\t\ts = strings.TrimRight(string(r[:maxQuoteRunes]), \"\\n\") + \"\\n… (truncated; full text in the committed file)\"\n\t}\n\tlines := strings.Split(s, \"\\n\")\n\tfor i, l := range lines {\n\t\tlines[i] = strings.TrimRight(\"> \"+l, \" \")\n\t}\n\treturn strings.Join(lines, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n// BodyInfo is what the PR body is made of.\ntype BodyInfo struct {\n\tRepo    github.Repo\n\tIssue   int\n\tRole    string\n\tBranch  string\n\tJobID   string\n\tSummary string // redacted agent text; \"\" when withheld or absent\n\t// Withheld says the secret scan held the summary back.\n\tWithheld bool\n\tResult   closeout.WorkerResult\n}\n\n// Body renders the PR body. The only closing reference in it is ard's own\n// \"Closes #<issue>\", written here and never passed through NeutraliseRefs.\nfunc Body(b BodyInfo) string {\n\tvar w strings.Builder\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"Closes #%d\\n\\n\", b.Issue)\n\tplan := fmt.Sprintf(\"https://github.com/%s/%s/blob/main/.ard/%d/plan.md\", b.Repo.Owner, b.Repo.Name, b.Issue)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"Opened by ard from issue #%d. The summary below was written by an agent and is untrusted; the [plan](%s) came from triage.\\n\\n\", b.Issue, plan)\n\tif b.Withheld {\n\t\tw.WriteString(\"> \" + summaryWithheld + \"\\n\")\n\t} else {\n\t\tw.WriteString(QuoteSummary(b.Summary) + \"\\n\")\n\t}\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"\\n**Tests:** %s\\n\", testsText(b.Result))\n\tif b.Result.Large != \"\" {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"\\n**%s**\\n\", b.Result.Large)\n\t}\n\tbase := fmt.Sprintf(\"https://github.com/%s/%s\", b.Repo.Owner, b.Repo.Name)\n\trole := fmt.Sprintf(\".ard/%d/worker-%s.md\", b.Issue, b.Role)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"\\n**Files:** [%s](%s/blob/%s/%s) · [logs](%s/tree/%s/.ard/%d/logs) · job `%s`\\n\",\n\t\trole, base, b.Branch, role, base, b.Branch, b.Issue, b.JobID)\n\treturn w.String()\n}\n\nfunc testsText(r closeout.WorkerResult) string {\n\tfixes := \"\"\n\tswitch r.Fixes {\n\tcase 0:\n\tcase 1:\n\t\tfixes = \" after 1 fix attempt\"\n\tdefault:\n\t\tfixes = fmt.Sprintf(\" after %d fix attempts\", r.Fixes)\n\t}\n\tswitch r.Tests {\n\tcase closeout.TestsPassed:\n\t\treturn \"passed\" + fixes\n\tcase closeout.TestsFailed:\n\t\ts := \"failed\" + fixes\n\t\tif r.TestNote != \"\" {\n\t\t\ts += \" (\" + r.TestNote + \")\"\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn s\n\t}\n\treturn \"not configured\"\n}\n44:func buildWorker(j Job, rf RoleFile, rs RoleSet) Plan {\n48:\t\treturn needsHumanPlan(j, rf.Status, \"worker ended without the loop's result\")\n50:\t\treturn needsHumanPlan(j, rf.Status, w.Hold)\n52:\t\treturn needsHumanPlan(j, rf.Status, \"worker finished without a pull request\")\n56:\tp := Plan{Status: rf.Status, Outcome: OutcomeDone}\n58:\tnow, deferred, skipped := resolveNext(j, rf.Next, rs)\n87:\tif rf.Worker != \"\" {\n88:\t\tp.Skipped = append(p.Skipped, Skip{\"worker:\" + clip(rf.Worker, 60), \"wrong stage\"})\n---\nrole: engineer\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\n---\nAdds a per-role network setting to the config loader and carries it through the executor Job and Handle, the agent environment, the host warning and the agent prompt, with a new config check warning. Nothing is enforced. The plan has 15 steps with tests for each part. Size M to L, risk low to medium.\n\n## Open issues\n- The plan renders the resolved config's network value as a string (\"true\", \"false (advisory)\", \"false (advisory, host)\") so the suffixes can be printed. A maintainer may prefer a separate field.\n- The worker's test step is left with network on, so only the agent step is marked advisory.\n236:**The docs scanner** (`scanners/docs`, a `sh` script plus one stdlib-only Go program, `docscan.go`, built at scan time with the job's own Go) lists undocumented exported Go identifiers. It reports, per package directory: a missing package comment; an exported function, a method on an exported receiver type (pointer, value or generic) and an exported type with no doc comment (the spec's doc, its trailing comment or the enclosing declaration's doc all count); and an exported const or var with no comment, one finding per parenthesised block that has no block comment. Struct fields, interface methods, methods on unexported types and comment wording are not checked. Every exported identifier counts, `internal/` included, and files for every platform count (build constraints are not evaluated). It skips `testdata/`, `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot or underscore directories, files whose names start with `_` or `.`, symlinks, `*_test.go`, `//go:build ignore` files, generated files and `package main`. The fingerprint is `docs:undoc:<dir>:<symbol>` or `docs:pkg:<dir>`; the file and line are left out, so moving a declaration within its package keeps its issue. Severity is `low` and the group is the package directory. It only parses files: it never runs repository code and needs no network. It fails closed: a parse error, unreadable file, invalid path or more than 10,000 findings fails the run with nothing filed. A human-owned `exclude` file in the role directory skips package directories (`dir/` is a directory and everything below it, anything else a glob matched against the package directory); an entry makes its findings vanish, so autoclose closes their issues. Go only for now.\n279:**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\n283:An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; agent jobs get only their model credential.\n335:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages and uses no other network, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make test`, and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base.\n388:  - when the rules cannot be read (a 5xx, a network error, a permissions 403), nothing merges that tick. Rule types ard does not evaluate (`workflows`, `code_scanning`, `required_deployments`) are logged at warn\n476:**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. In this part it is advisory only and nothing is enforced: a role with `network: false` gets `ARD_NETWORK=0` (else `1`) in its job environment and a constant \"Network: unavailable.\" (else \"Network: available.\") line in the agent's user message. The value rides on the executor job and handle from config only, never from anything the agent can read or change. In host mode the controller logs that `network: false` is not enforced. `ard config check` prints `false (advisory)`, or `false (advisory, host)` for a host-mode role, and warns when a host-mode role that reads untrusted input has `network: true`, naming the GitHub App private key file as reachable.\n490:- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token; in Kubernetes a network policy limits egress to the model API and package registries.\n**A deterministic gate starts the worker.** Once per triage tick, after the intake sweep, the controller searches for open issues that carry `ard:triage:lead:done` and none of `ard:pending`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` or any configured worker's request label (the exclusion keeps gated issues from filling the 100-hit window). It re-reads each hit and decides from labels alone. \"Requested\" means the request label is present, \"done\" means `:done` is present. The gate waits while any triage job of the issue is running or any requested `ard:triage:<role>` lacks its `:done`; it leaves an issue alone once any `ard:worker:<role>` label exists (a worker already gated, started by a human, or in a fix round). When triage is finished it adds the lead's chosen `ard:worker:<role>`. The choice is read from the lead's close-out record in controller state, the value close-out validated, not from the committed `triage-lead.md`. It needs no pool slot and runs in the same conditions as the rest of the tick.\n\nThree cases go to a human, each with one comment and `ard:needs-human`, and nothing is ever closed automatically: the lead finished `done` but named no valid worker (or its close-out record is missing), the chosen worker role no longer exists, or an `ard:triage:<role>` is requested for a role that does not exist. A title or body edit after admission, on a human-filed issue, also stops the gate with a \"paused\" comment and `ard:needs-human`; re-admission by relabel is not offered, since a lead with `:done` is never re-claimed. A maintainer re-runs triage or adds the worker label.\n\nBefore it asks for approval or starts a worker, the gate checks that `.ard/<n>/plan.md` exists on `main`. If it does not, the gate posts one comment, adds `ard:needs-human` and records a `gate` event with reason `no-plan`; a check that fails to run is retried on the next tick. Removing a role's request label by hand does not count as that role finishing. With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\n\n**Order comes from config, not the lead.** A role can declare `after: [architect, product-manager]`; the controller holds its request until those roles, if requested, are done. A prerequisite is requested when its request label (`ard:pending` for the lead) or its `:running` label is present, and done when its `:done` label is present; one that was never requested does not hold. A held role is logged as `triage.held`, is not a candidate and does not count against the one-triage-job-per-issue rule. Config rejects a role that is after itself and any cycle. Engineer triage normally runs last so its steps reflect the design and requirements.\n\n| Role | Adds to plan.md | Typical trigger |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lead | nothing (role file only); writes `triage-lead.md` with routing, or `needs-human` when a person must decide | every `ard:pending` issue |\nHost log files older than 7 days are pruned hourly. The Reaper keeps running while the controller is paused.\n\nSuccessful jobs clean up at close-out: the worktree is removed, the Kubernetes `Job` gets a short TTL, and logs stay 7 days.\n\nHost job steps run with `GOFLAGS=-modcacherw`, so a Go module cache downloaded into the job home is writable. The module cache is per job and is not shared, because a cache that jobs can write to would let one job poison modules that later jobs build. Cleanup restores owner write permission on the whole job home and worktree before removing them, and never follows symlinks, so read-only trees no longer leak.\n\n## Worker loop\nbudget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo, UTC day, blocks at spent >= cap; a role may set its own; provisional, recalibrated between Gate 1 and Gate 2\nmax_budget_usd: 5            # per job; provisional, same recalibration\nglobal_max_open: 30          # open scanner issues across all scanners\npaused: false\nintervals: { scanners: 4h, triage: 15m, workers: 5m, reviewers: 5m, merger: 5m, prwatch: 5m }\npr_watch: { pending_timeout: 2h }   # at least intervals.prwatch, at most 7d\nintake: { mode: label }     # mode: all | label; label (label mode only) defaults to ard:pending, must start with ard: and must not be one of ard's own labels\nalpine-linux.json\nangular.json\ndebian.json\ngo.json\nnginx.json\nnodejs.json\npython.json\nredis.json\nubuntu.json\n9:test:\n10-\tgo test ./...\n11-\n12-lint:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fe75d909-a730-40c7-a147-43d1c2a12ca1","uuid":"21745fa0-e994-403d-9755-1be737325262","timestamp":"2026-11-24T19:18:26.497Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"package worker\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/closeout\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/committer\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/github\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tmaxQuoteRunes     = 4000\n\tsummaryWithheld   = \"Summary withheld by the secret scan.\"\n\tsummaryNone       = \"The agent wrote no summary.\"\n\ttestsRanOutOfTime = \"ran out of time\"\n)\n\n// Title is the PR title: the first line of the code commit message.\nfunc Title(typ, issueTitle string, issue int, jobID string) (string, error) {\n\tmsg, err := committer.CodeMessage(typ, issueTitle, issue, \"\", jobID)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\tfirst, _, _ := strings.Cut(msg, \"\\n\")\n\treturn first, nil\n}\n\n// QuoteSummary renders agent text for a PR body: closing references\n// neutralised (including the HTML-entity and backslash forms of \"#\"), ard\n// markers and @-mentions defused, cut to 4,000 runes and quoted. The text must\n// already be redacted.\nfunc QuoteSummary(s string) string {\n\ts = strings.TrimSpace(strings.ReplaceAll(s, \"\\r\", \"\"))\n\tif s == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"> \" + summaryNone\n\t}\n\ts = closeout.Sanitize(committer.NeutraliseRefs(s))\n\tif r := []rune(s); len(r) > maxQuoteRunes {\n\t\ts = strings.TrimRight(string(r[:maxQuoteRunes]), \"\\n\") + \"\\n… (truncated; full text in the committed file)\"\n\t}\n\tlines := strings.Split(s, \"\\n\")\n\tfor i, l := range lines {\n\t\tlines[i] = strings.TrimRight(\"> \"+l, \" \")\n\t}\n\treturn strings.Join(lines, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n// BodyInfo is what the PR body is made of.\ntype BodyInfo struct {\n\tRepo    github.Repo\n\tIssue   int\n\tRole    string\n\tBranch  string\n\tJobID   string\n\tSummary string // redacted agent text; \"\" when withheld or absent\n\t// Withheld says the secret scan held the summary back.\n\tWithheld bool\n\tResult   closeout.WorkerResult\n}\n\n// Body renders the PR body. The only closing reference in it is ard's own\n// \"Closes #<issue>\", written here and never passed through NeutraliseRefs.\nfunc Body(b BodyInfo) string {\n\tvar w strings.Builder\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"Closes #%d\\n\\n\", b.Issue)\n\tplan := fmt.Sprintf(\"https://github.com/%s/%s/blob/main/.ard/%d/plan.md\", b.Repo.Owner, b.Repo.Name, b.Issue)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"Opened by ard from issue #%d. The summary below was written by an agent and is untrusted; the [plan](%s) came from triage.\\n\\n\", b.Issue, plan)\n\tif b.Withheld {\n\t\tw.WriteString(\"> \" + summaryWithheld + \"\\n\")\n\t} else {\n\t\tw.WriteString(QuoteSummary(b.Summary) + \"\\n\")\n\t}\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"\\n**Tests:** %s\\n\", testsText(b.Result))\n\tif b.Result.Large != \"\" {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"\\n**%s**\\n\", b.Result.Large)\n\t}\n\tbase := fmt.Sprintf(\"https://github.com/%s/%s\", b.Repo.Owner, b.Repo.Name)\n\trole := fmt.Sprintf(\".ard/%d/worker-%s.md\", b.Issue, b.Role)\n\tfmt.Fprintf(&w, \"\\n**Files:** [%s](%s/blob/%s/%s) · [logs](%s/tree/%s/.ard/%d/logs) · job `%s`\\n\",\n\t\trole, base, b.Branch, role, base, b.Branch, b.Issue, b.JobID)\n\treturn w.String()\n}\n\nfunc testsText(r closeout.WorkerResult) string {\n\tfixes := \"\"\n\tswitch r.Fixes {\n\tcase 0:\n\tcase 1:\n\t\tfixes = \" after 1 fix attempt\"\n\tdefault:\n\t\tfixes = fmt.Sprintf(\" after %d fix attempts\", r.Fixes)\n\t}\n\tswitch r.Tests {\n\tcase closeout.TestsPassed:\n\t\treturn \"passed\" + fixes\n\tcase closeout.TestsFailed:\n\t\ts := \"failed\" + fixes\n\t\tif r.TestNote != \"\" {\n\t\t\ts += \" (\" + r.TestNote + \")\"\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn s\n\t}\n\treturn \"not configured\"\n}\n44:func buildWorker(j Job, rf RoleFile, rs RoleSet) Plan {\n48:\t\treturn needsHumanPlan(j, rf.Status, \"worker ended without the loop's result\")\n50:\t\treturn needsHumanPlan(j, rf.Status, w.Hold)\n52:\t\treturn needsHumanPlan(j, rf.Status, \"worker finished without a pull request\")\n56:\tp := Plan{Status: rf.Status, Outcome: OutcomeDone}\n58:\tnow, deferred, skipped := resolveNext(j, rf.Next, rs)\n87:\tif rf.Worker != \"\" {\n88:\t\tp.Skipped = append(p.Skipped, Skip{\"worker:\" + clip(rf.Worker, 60), \"wrong stage\"})\n---\nrole: engineer\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\n---\nAdds a per-role network setting to the config loader and carries it through the executor Job and Handle, the agent environment, the host warning and the agent prompt, with a new config check warning. Nothing is enforced. The plan has 15 steps with tests for each part. Size M to L, risk low to medium.\n\n## Open issues\n- The plan renders the resolved config's network value as a string (\"true\", \"false (advisory)\", \"false (advisory, host)\") so the suffixes can be printed. A maintainer may prefer a separate field.\n- The worker's test step is left with network on, so only the agent step is marked advisory.\n236:**The docs scanner** (`scanners/docs`, a `sh` script plus one stdlib-only Go program, `docscan.go`, built at scan time with the job's own Go) lists undocumented exported Go identifiers. It reports, per package directory: a missing package comment; an exported function, a method on an exported receiver type (pointer, value or generic) and an exported type with no doc comment (the spec's doc, its trailing comment or the enclosing declaration's doc all count); and an exported const or var with no comment, one finding per parenthesised block that has no block comment. Struct fields, interface methods, methods on unexported types and comment wording are not checked. Every exported identifier counts, `internal/` included, and files for every platform count (build constraints are not evaluated). It skips `testdata/`, `vendor/`, `node_modules/` and dot or underscore directories, files whose names start with `_` or `.`, symlinks, `*_test.go`, `//go:build ignore` files, generated files and `package main`. The fingerprint is `docs:undoc:<dir>:<symbol>` or `docs:pkg:<dir>`; the file and line are left out, so moving a declaration within its package keeps its issue. Severity is `low` and the group is the package directory. It only parses files: it never runs repository code and needs no network. It fails closed: a parse error, unreadable file, invalid path or more than 10,000 findings fails the run with nothing filed. A human-owned `exclude` file in the role directory skips package directories (`dir/` is a directory and everything below it, anything else a glob matched against the package directory); an entry makes its findings vanish, so autoclose closes their issues. Go only for now.\n279:**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\n283:An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; agent jobs get only their model credential.\n335:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages and uses no other network, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make test`, and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base.\n388:  - when the rules cannot be read (a 5xx, a network error, a permissions 403), nothing merges that tick. Rule types ard does not evaluate (`workflows`, `code_scanning`, `required_deployments`) are logged at warn\n476:**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. In this part it is advisory only and nothing is enforced: a ro