d-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\ndocs/proposal.md:234:**The trufflehog-history scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog-history`) runs `trufflehog git file://$ARD_HISTORY --bare` with the same flags plus no `--since-commit`, `--max-depth` or `--branch`, over every fetched ref, with the same hash salt, so a value has the same fingerprint as at HEAD. A scanner role asks for history by holding an empty file named `history` in its directory (`Role.ReadsHistory`; there is no config key, so an override cannot grant it). The executor then sets `Job.History` (scanner stage only) and mounts the controller's bare clone read-only at `/history` in Docker (host mode uses the clone path) and sets `ARD_HISTORY`. The mount includes unmerged PR branches. Commit, author and timestamp are dropped; only the detector, file, line and hash leave the scan. It never autocloses, because history does not go away. A Kubernetes executor must provide a read-only copy at the same place.\ndocs/proposal.md:279:**Sidecar repos are context, read-only.** A role's sidecar (for example `vpe-specs`) is cloned by the executor, not the loop: the loop passes the role's resolved sidecar (repo and ref) on the job. The executor keeps one shallow (depth 1) bare object cache per sidecar repo at `~/.ard/sidecars/<controlled-owner>/<controlled-repo>/<sidecar-owner>/<sidecar-repo>.git`, shared by every job of that controlled repo, and exports the commit into the job home as `<job home>/sidecar` (plain files: no `.git`, `chmod -R a-w` in both modes, and a `:ro` mount under Docker). The job sees it at `$ARD_SIDECAR`. `ref` is a branch, tag or full 40-character SHA (an abbreviated SHA is a config error). A SHA already in the cache needs no network; a branch or tag is re-fetched (depth 1) for every job. Whatever the ref, `Prepare` resolves it to a full commit SHA and records it on the job's handle (and in the `executor.sidecar_ready` log), so a job is reproducible. Symlinks and submodules in the sidecar are rejected and the tree is capped at 64 MiB, like config snapshots. A fetch failure (network, auth, a stale lock, which the executor clears by rebuilding the cache once) is the transient `clone_failed`; a ref or SHA that does not exist, or a repo the App cannot read, is a permanent failure that names the sidecar. A role that wants a spec changed says so in `plan.md`; editing the sidecar is out of scope for v1.\ndocs/proposal.md:326:2. **Branch** `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` from `origin/main`. The slug is the issue title lowercased, every run of other characters turned into one `-`, at most 40 ASCII characters, `issue` when nothing is left. The title can change between rounds, so the loop looks the branch up with `git ls-remote` on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-*`: one match is reused (and fetched), none is created on the first push, and more than one (only a human can make the second) posts once and adds `ard:needs-human` without claiming. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so `worker/<role>-<issue#>-` names exactly one role and issue.\ndocs/proposal.md:419:**Shared roles.** `roles_repo: {repo: <owner>/<name>, ref: <ref>}` in `.ard/config.yaml` loads role directories from another repository, so several repositories can reuse one role set. It is pinned like a sidecar: `ref` defaults to the shared repository's default branch (its remote HEAD, so `master` works too) and may be a branch, a tag or a full commit SHA. The controller fetches the ref shallowly, merges the shared repository's `.ard/scanners`, `triage`, `workers` and `reviewers` directories into the config snapshot, and a directory of the same kind and name in this repository wins whole, never file by file. `overrides` apply to shared roles too. `ard serve` re-reads the key with the rest of the config. If the shared repository cannot be fetched, or a shared role is invalid, the config fails to load and the controller counts as paused. Trust: the key lives in `.ard/config.yaml`, a protected path, but a branch or tag in the shared repository can change the prompts agents run without a PR here, so pin a full commit SHA.\ndocs/proposal.md:482:**Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA replac